amiliar cases, explain how it handles dependencies and counterfactuals, and indicate where the source is under-specified. Some of the formal refinements below—especially the use of total evidence, zero-probability actions, policy evaluation, and ratifiability—are my own elaborations rather than claims made explicitly by the source.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The basic EDT rule\n\nSuppose an agent faces a finite set of possible actions\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A=\\{A_1,\\dots,A_n\\}\n\\]\n\nand a finite set of possible outcomes\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal O=\\{O_1,\\dots,O_m\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe agent has a probability function \\(P\\) representing her beliefs, and a desirability or utility function \\(D\\) assigning a real number \\(D(O_j)\\) to each outcome. EDT defines the value of an action \\(A\\) as the conditional expected desirability of outcomes given that \\(A\\) is performed:\n\n\\[\nV(A)=\\sum_j P(O_j\\mid A)D(O_j).\n\\]\n\nEquivalently,\n\n\\[\nV(A)=\\mathbb E_P[D(O)\\mid A].\n\\]\n\nThe EDT prescription is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Choose } A^* \\in \\arg\\max_{A\\in\\mathcal A} V(A).\n\\]\n\nThis is the formula given in the reference article, attributed to Gibbard and Harper (1976). The article emphasizes that \\(D(O_j)\\) is the desirability of outcome \\(O_j\\), while \\(P(O_j\\mid A)\\) is the ordinary conditional probability of \\(O_j\\) given that action \\(A\\) occurs.\n\nIn most realistic applications one should include the agent’s total background evidence \\(E\\). A more explicit version is therefore\n\n\\[\nV_E(A)=\\sum_j P(O_j\\mid A,E)D(O_j).\n\\]\n\nThis refinement is not stated in the source, but it is natural: EDT evaluates the news value of an action relative to what the agent already knows.\n\nIf outcomes are determined by an action together with a state of the world \\(S\\), we can write\n\n\\[\nV(A)=\\sum_s P(S=s\\mid A,E)U(A,s),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(U(A,s)\\) is the utility of performing \\(A\\) in state \\(s\\). The crucial point is that EDT conditions the probability of states on the action. If performing \\(A\\) is evidence that the world is in a good state, EDT counts that in favor of \\(A\\).\n\n---\n\n## 2. EDT versus CDT\n\nThe reference article contrasts EDT with causal decision theory. CDT uses counterfactual probabilities:\n\n\\[\nU(A)=\\sum_j P(A \\mathrel{\\Box\\rightarrow} O_j)D(O_j),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(A \\mathrel{\\Box\\rightarrow} O_j\\) means something like “if \\(A\\) were performed, \\(O_j\\) would obtain.” In modern causal-model language, CDT often evaluates\n\n\\[\nU(A)=\\sum_j P(O_j\\mid do(A))D(O_j),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(do(A)\\) represents an intervention that sets the action to \\(A\\). EDT instead uses the observational conditional\n\n\\[\nV(A)=\\sum_j P(O_j\\mid A)D(O_j).\n\\]\n\nThe difference is important whenever the action is correlated with outcomes for reasons other than direct causation. For example, an action may be predicted by a highly accurate predictor, caused by a common factor, or correlated with another agent’s action because the agents reason similarly. EDT treats all such probabilistic dependencies as decision-relevant. CDT asks whether the action itself would make the outcome more likely; EDT asks whether the action is good news.\n\nThis is why the source says that EDT and CDT agree in many cases but diverge in certain philosophical thought experiments. When action and outcome are related only by ordinary causation, and there are no confounding correlations, the conditional \\(P(O_j\\mid A)\\) and the counterfactual/interventional probability \\(P(A\\mathrel{\\Box\\rightarrow}O_j)\\) often coincide. They come apart in cases involving prediction, duplication, or common causes.\n\n---\n\n## 3. What counts as a relevant dependency?\n\nEDT’s central commitment is that any probabilistic dependence between action and outcome can matter, provided it remains after conditioning on the agent’s evidence. This includes:\n\n1. **Direct causal dependence.** If smoking causes cancer, then \\(P(\\text{cancer}\\mid \\text{smoking})\\) reflects a causal relation, and EDT will disfavor smoking if cancer is bad.\n\n2. **Predictive dependence.** If a predictor has already predicted what you will do, your action may be strong evidence about the predictor’s earlier prediction.\n\n3. **Common-cause dependence.** If some underlying condition causes both your action and an outcome, your action may be evidence of that outcome even if it does not cause it.\n\n4. **Logical or algorithmic correlation.** If another agent uses the same reasoning process as you, your choice may be evidence of their choice, even without communication.\n\nThe reference article illustrates predictive dependence with Newcomb’s paradox and algorithmic correlation with the twin prisoner’s dilemma. EDT does not, by itself, ask whether the dependence is causal. It only asks what the conditional probabilities are.\n\nThis breadth is also the source of the main objections to EDT. To see why, consider an example not mentioned in the source but often used in discussions of EDT.\n\n### A common-cause example: the smoking lesion\n\nSuppose a genetic lesion causes both a tendency to smoke and a high risk of cancer. Smoking itself does not cause cancer. Suppose also that smoking gives no direct pleasure. The agent knows only the statistical association:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{cancer}\\mid \\text{smoke})=0.9,\n\\]\n\\[\nP(\\text{cancer}\\mid \\text{do not smoke})=0.1.\n\\]\n\nLet cancer have utility \\(-100\\), and no cancer have utility \\(0\\). Then EDT gives\n\n\\[\nV(\\text{smoke})=0.9(-100)+0.1(0)=-90,\n\\]\n\nwhile\n\n\\[\nV(\\text{do not smoke})=0.1(-100)+0.9(0)=-10.\n\\]\n\nEDT recommends not smoking, because smoking is bad news: it indicates that one probably has the lesion and hence cancer. But if smoking does not causally affect cancer, CDT would not reject smoking on those grounds. This example is not in the source, but it clarifies the dispute. Critics such as Lewis and Joyce, cited in the article, object that EDT encourages “managing the news” rather than producing good outcomes.\n\nOne possible EDT response is that if the agent learns she has or lacks the lesion, the relevant conditional probabilities change. If she knows she has the lesion, smoking may no longer be evidence of cancer beyond what she already knows. Thus EDT’s verdict depends sensitively on the agent’s total evidence.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Newcomb’s paradox\n\nThe reference article’s central example is Newcomb’s paradox. There is a predictor, a player, and two boxes:\n\n- Box A is transparent and always contains \\$1,000.\n- Box B is opaque. Its contents were already determined by the predictor.\n- If the predictor predicted that the player will take both boxes, box B is empty.\n- If the predictor predicted that the player will take only box B, box B contains \\$1,000,000.\n\nThe player can either take both boxes or take only box B. The predictor is highly reliable; the article uses 99% accuracy.\n\nEDT recommends taking only box B. The reason is that taking only box B is strong evidence that the predictor predicted one-boxing, in which case box B contains \\$1,000,000. Taking both boxes is strong evidence that the predictor predicted two-boxing, in which case box B is empty.\n\nUsing the article’s numbers:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(\\text{take only B})\n&=P(\\text{1M in B}\\mid \\text{take only B})\\cdot \\$1{,}000{,}000 \\\\\n&\\quad + P(\\text{nothing in B}\\mid \\text{take only B})\\cdot \\$0 \\\\\n&=0.99\\cdot \\$1{,}000{,}000+0.01\\cdot \\$0 \\\\\n&=\\$990{,}000.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFor two-boxing:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(\\text{take both})\n&=P(\\text{1M in B}\\mid \\text{take both})\\cdot \\$1{,}001{,}000 \\\\\n&\\quad + P(\\text{nothing in B}\\mid \\text{take both})\\cdot \\$1{,}000 \\\\\n&=0.01\\cdot \\$1{,}001{,}000+0.99\\cdot \\$1{,}000 \\\\\n&=\\$11{,}000.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\$990{,}000>\\$11{,}000\\), EDT says: take only box B.\n\nCDT, according to the source, recommends taking both boxes. The causal reasoning is that the predictor has already acted. The contents of box B are fixed. Taking both boxes cannot change whether box B contains \\$1,000,000; it only adds the sure \\$1,000 from box A. Thus two-boxing causally dominates one-boxing: in every possible state of box B, taking both yields \\$1,000 more than taking only B. EDT nevertheless one-boxes because it conditions on the evidential connection between the player’s choice and the predictor’s earlier prediction.\n\nA useful extension, not explicitly in the source, is to compute the threshold predictor accuracy required for EDT to one-box. Let \\(p\\) be the probability that the predictor predicts one-boxing given that the player one-boxes, and also the probability that it predicts two-boxing given that the player two-boxes. Let \\(M=1{,}000{,}000\\). Then\n\n\\[\nV(\\text{one-box})=pM,\n\\]\n\nwhile\n\n\\[\nV(\\text{two-box})=(1-p)(M+1000)+p(1000)=(1-p)M+1000.\n\\]\n\nEDT prefers one-boxing when\n\n\\[\npM>(1-p)M+1000,\n\\]\n\nor\n\n\\[\n(2p-1)M>1000.\n\\]\n\nWith \\(M=1{,}000{,}000\\), this requires\n\n\\[\np>0.5005.\n\\]\n\nSo EDT one-boxes even if the predictor is only barely better than chance, provided the million-dollar reward is large enough relative to the sure thousand dollars.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The twin prisoner’s dilemma\n\nThe reference article also discusses a variant of the prisoner’s dilemma involving psychological twins. Aomame and her twin are in separate rooms and cannot communicate. Their payoffs are:\n\n- Both cooperate: each gets \\$5.\n- Both defect: each gets \\$1.\n- One cooperates, one defects: defector gets \\$10, cooperator gets \\$0.\n\nThe article assumes that the twin’s reasoning processes are exactly analogous to Aomame’s. EDT then says that Aomame’s choice is strong evidence about her twin’s choice. If Aomame cooperates, that is evidence that her twin will cooperate. If she defects, that is evidence that her twin will defect.\n\nIn the idealized case of perfect correlation:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(\\text{cooperate})\n&=P(\\text{twin cooperates}\\mid \\text{Aomame cooperates})\\cdot \\$5 \\\\\n&\\quad +P(\\text{twin defects}\\mid \\text{Aomame cooperates})\\cdot \\$0 \\\\\n&=1\\cdot \\$5+0\\cdot \\$0 \\\\\n&=\\$5,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nwhile\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(\\text{defect})\n&=P(\\text{twin cooperates}\\mid \\text{Aomame defects})\\cdot \\$10 \\\\\n&\\quad +P(\\text{twin defects}\\mid \\text{Aomame defects})\\cdot \\$1 \\\\\n&=0\\cdot \\$10+1\\cdot \\$1 \\\\\n&=\\$1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEDT therefore recommends cooperation.\n\nAgain, it is helpful to generalize. Suppose the correlation is imperfect. Let\n\n\\[\nq=P(\\text{twin cooperates}\\mid \\text{Aomame cooperates})\n\\]\n\nand suppose symmetrically that\n\n\\[\nq=P(\\text{twin defects}\\mid \\text{Aomame defects}).\n\\]\n\nThen\n\n\\[\nV(\\text{cooperate})=5q,\n\\]\n\nwhile\n\n\\[\nV(\\text{defect})=10(1-q)+q=10-9q.\n\\]\n\nCooperation is recommended when\n\n\\[\n5q>10-9q,\n\\]\n\nor\n\n\\[\n14q>10,\n\\]\n\nso\n\n\\[\nq>\\frac{5}{7}\\approx 0.714.\n\\]\n\nThus EDT cooperates when the twin is sufficiently likely to make the same choice, but defects if the correlation is weak. This illustrates how EDT can be applied to unfamiliar cases: estimate the conditional probabilities induced by the relevant dependence, multiply by utilities, and compare.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Policies, not merely isolated actions\n\nThe reference article mostly discusses one-shot actions, but EDT can also evaluate policies. A policy is a rule mapping circumstances to actions. Let \\(\\pi\\) be a policy, and let \\([\\pi]\\) be the proposition that the agent adopts or executes \\(\\pi\\). Then EDT can define\n\n\\[\nV(\\pi)=\\mathbb E[D(O)\\mid \\pi]\n=\\sum_j P(O_j\\mid \\pi)D(O_j).\n\\]\n\nIn sequential problems, the relevant “action” may be an entire plan. For example, an agent might choose between a policy of one-boxing in Newcomb-like situations and a policy of two-boxing. EDT evaluates the policy by conditioning on the proposition that the policy is followed.\n\nThis matters because EDT can sometimes recommend ex ante commitment to a policy even if, at a later moment, a myopic act-by-act calculation might look different. If the agent can bind herself, or if predictors anticipate her general policy, then the policy is the proper object of evaluation. If she cannot bind herself and will re-decide later using EDT, then EDT may need to evaluate each future choice separately. This creates possible dynamic inconsistency.\n\nThe source does not fully address this issue. A reasonable operational interpretation, which I propose rather than find in the article, is this: EDT should be applied to the option that is genuinely under the agent’s control at the time of decision. If the agent is choosing a binding policy, evaluate the policy as a whole. If she is choosing only an immediate act, evaluate that act conditional on current evidence. If there is a conflict between ex ante policy evaluation and later act-by-act evaluation, the case should specify whether commitment is possible. EDT by itself does not automatically solve all sequential-choice problems.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Counterfactuals and zero-probability actions\n\nA technical subtlety arises because EDT uses conditional probabilities \\(P(O_j\\mid A)\\). Ordinary conditioning requires \\(P(A)>0\\). But in deterministic deliberation, an agent may regard some actions as impossible. For instance, if she is certain she will choose the action with highest expected utility, then before deliberation she may assign probability zero to inferior actions. Conditioning on those actions then becomes problematic.\n\nThe reference article does not discuss this. One operational solution is to assign small positive probabilities, sometimes called “trembles,” to every possible action. For example, suppose each action has at least probability \\(\\epsilon>0\\). Then compute EDT values using those probabilities and take the limit as \\(\\epsilon\\to 0\\) if needed. Another approach is to treat conditional probabilities as primitive, not defined by the ratio \\(P(O\\land A)/P(A)\\). A third approach is to model the agent’s decision procedure explicitly, including uncertainty about how she will reason, and then condition on action events within that enlarged model.\n\nThese moves are not mere technicalities. EDT is a theory about what an agent should do given her beliefs, but those beliefs include beliefs about her own possible choices. The source’s simple formula assumes that such conditional probabilities are available. In applying EDT to unfamiliar cases, one must therefore specify a probability model over actions, states, predictors, and outcomes.\n\nA related issue is ratifiability. An EDT choice might be self-undermining if the agent’s recognition that she is making that choice changes the evidential probabilities. A natural strengthening, not stated in the source, is to require that an EDT choice be *ratifiable*: it should remain optimal conditional on the agent’s knowledge that she is choosing by EDT. Formally, if \\(R\\) is the proposition that the agent is using EDT, one may require\n\n\\[\nA^*\\in \\arg\\max_A \\sum_j P(O_j\\mid A,R,E)D(O_j).\n\\]\n\nThis is one way to make EDT more stable in reflexive decision problems. Whether EDT should be supplemented in this way is philosophically contested, and the reference article leaves the question open.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Sleeping Beauty and indexical uncertainty\n\nThe reference article briefly mentions the Sleeping Beauty problem. It says that EDT has been applied to Sleeping Beauty in order to avoid Dutch book arguments, but that Vincent Conitzer has argued that “halfers” can still be Dutch-booked even after adopting EDT (Conitzer, 2015).\n\nThe important general lesson is that EDT is not, by itself, a complete theory of self-locating belief. To apply EDT in Sleeping Beauty, one must first decide how Beauty should assign credences upon awakening: for example, whether heads should receive credence \\(1/2\\) or \\(1/3\\). EDT then evaluates bets or choices conditional on the relevant awakening event. Different prior accounts of indexical evidence can therefore lead to different EDT recommendations. The source correctly presents this as an area of ongoing dispute rather than a settled application of EDT.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Philosophical motivation and criticism\n\nEDT has an attractive interpretation: it tells agents to choose the action that would be the best news. If you imagine receiving a message saying “you performed \\(A\\),” EDT asks which such message would make you most pleased, given your beliefs and desires. This makes EDT especially natural in environments where choices are predictable or correlated. If many similar agents use similar reasoning, your choice may be evidence about their choices, and therefore about large-scale outcomes.\n\nThe reference article mentions a supporting argument along these lines: even if one is not fully confident in EDT, it may be reasonable to act as if EDT were true because EDT can involve the actions of many correlated decision-makers, giving its recommendations high stakes (MacAskill et al., 2021). In such cases, the evidential relevance of one’s action may extend far beyond its local causal impact.\n\nCritics reject this emphasis on news value. The article quotes David Lewis’s characterization of EDT as “an irrational policy of managing the news” (Lewis, 1981). James Joyce is similarly quoted as saying that rational agents choose acts on the basis of their causal efficacy, not their auspiciousness; they act to bring about good results even when doing so might betoken bad news (Joyce, 1999).\n\nThe disagreement is deep. EDT says that if an action is reliably associated with good outcomes, that association is decision-relevant. CDT says that only the action’s causal contribution is decision-relevant. In Newcomb’s paradox, EDT one-boxes because one-boxing is evidence that the predictor filled box B with a million dollars. CDT two-boxes because the predictor’s act is already finished, so the player can only add the extra \\$1,000. In the twin prisoner’s dilemma, EDT cooperates because cooperation is evidence that the twin will cooperate. CDT may defect if it treats the twin’s action as causally independent.\n\nThe smoking-lesion case shows why many philosophers resist EDT: it can recommend avoiding a harmless symptom of bad outcomes. EDT defenders may respond that such cases are misleading, or that a properly formulated EDT with full evidence avoids some pathological verdicts. But the reference article’s criticisms section makes clear that the central philosophical dispute remains unresolved.\n\n---\n\n## 10. How to apply EDT in practice\n\nA reader wanting to apply EDT to an unfamiliar decision problem can use the following procedure:\n\n1. **Identify the available actions.**  \n   Make them mutually exclusive and exhaustive, or specify the relevant policy options.\n\n2. **Identify the possible outcomes.**  \n   Assign each outcome a desirability \\(D(O_j)\\). If utilities depend on states and actions, use \\(U(A,s)\\).\n\n3. **Specify the agent’s evidence.**  \n   Include all relevant background information \\(E\\).\n\n4. **Assign conditional probabilities.**  \n   Estimate \\(P(O_j\\mid A,E)\\) for each action and outcome. These probabilities should reflect all known causal, predictive, common-cause, or algorithmic dependencies.\n\n5. **Compute EDT values.**  \n   For each action,\n\n   \\[\n   V_E(A)=\\sum_j P(O_j\\mid A,E)D(O_j).\n   \\]\n\n6. **Choose the action with maximum EDT value.**  \n   If evaluating policies, replace \\(A\\) with a policy \\(\\pi\\) and condition on the policy being followed.\n\n7. **Check stability if needed.**  \n   In reflexive or sequential problems, ask whether the recommendation remains optimal when the agent conditions on the fact that she is using EDT, or whether a different policy should be adopted ex ante.\n\nThis procedure is simple, but its difficulty lies in step 4. EDT does not tell you where the conditional probabilities come from. It requires a coherent probabilistic model of the world, including the agent’s own possible choices.\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion\n\nEvidential decision theory offers a clean and powerful rule: choose the action that would be the best news. Its formal core is the conditional expected utility formula\n\n\\[\nV(A)=\\sum_j P(O_j\\mid A)D(O_j),\n\\]\n\nand its distinctive philosophical move is to treat all probabilistic dependencies between actions and outcomes as potentially decision-relevant. This explains why EDT one-boxes in Newcomb’s paradox and cooperates in the twin prisoner’s dilemma. It also explains why EDT is controversial: it can favor actions because they are symptoms of good outcomes, not because they cause those outcomes.\n\nThe theory is easy to state but subtle to apply. One must specify the relevant actions, outcomes, utilities, and especially the conditional probabilities given total evidence. In sequential, self-referential, or zero-probability cases, EDT may need supplementary ideas such as policy evaluation, trembling-hand probabilities, or ratifiability. Even with those refinements, the central dispute between EDT and causal decision theory remains: should rational choice track the best evidence or the best causal intervention? EDT gives a clear answer—choose what you would most want to learn you had chosen.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\nAhmed, A. (2021). *Evidential Decision Theory*. Cambridge University Press.\n\nConitzer, V. (2015). A Dutch book against sleeping beauties who are evidential decision theorists. *Synthese*, 192(9), 2887–2899.\n\nGibbard, A., & Harper, W. L. (1976). *Counterfactuals and Two Kinds of Expected Utility*.\n\nJoyce, J. M. (1999). *The Foundations of Causal Decision Theory*. Cambridge University Press.\n\nLewis, D. (1981). Causal decision theory. *Australasian Journal of Philosophy*, 59(1), 5–30.\n\nMacAskill, W., Vallinder, A., Österheld, C., Shulman, C., & Treutlein, J. (2021). *The Evidentialist’s Wager*.\n\nWikipedia contributors. (2026). Evidential decision theory. Wikipedia, revision 1373679688. https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Evidential_decision_theory&oldid=1373679688","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need answer user's request: write a mathematical essay/blog post explaining EDT and applying to decision problems. Target 2400-3000 words including references. Need coherent, precise enough for reader to decide unfamiliar cases. Explain how theory evaluates actions/policies, dependencies, counterfactuals. Where source leaves important question open, explain uncertainty and propose operational interpretation if reasonable. Distinguish source claims vs own assumptions. Use examples. Avoid unnecessary repetition, lengthy bibliographies, appendices. Finish brief conclusion. Source is Wikipedia article on Evidential decision theory. Need likely cite source and maybe references from it. Need produce final essay.\n\nNeed think deeply about EDT. Need define formal decision theory: states, actions, outcomes, utility/desirability, probability function. EDT value V(A)=sum_j P(O_j|A)D(O_j). Choose action maximizing V. News value: do what you most want to learn you will do. Contrast CDT U(A)=sum P(A □→ O_j)D(O_j). Need discuss conditional probabilities vs counterfactuals, dependency. EDT treats all probabilistic dependencies as decision-relevant, whether causal, common-cause, logical/evidential, predictor correlations. It conditions on the action as evidence. But action may be impossible/probability zero; need regularity or conditional probabilities as primitive / Jeffrey conditioning? Need mention acts with zero prior probability and conditional desirability maybe. Need policies: if policy is chosen, condition on event \"agent adopts/executes policy π\". For dynamic problems, EDT can evaluate policies by conditioning on entire policy; may face dynamic inconsistency if later choices re-evaluate? Need discuss. Need apply to Newcomb, twin prisoner's dilemma, maybe smoking lesion? Source doesn't mention smoking lesion but common criticism. Could include as own example? It would help explain dependency vs counterfactual. But need be careful: user wants use examples whenever helpful. We can include smoking lesion to show EDT's potential pathology: lesion causes both smoking and cancer, smoking evidence of cancer but doesn't cause it; EDT might avoid smoking if cancer bad, CDT doesn't. This illustrates dependency treatment and open questions about spurious correlation. But source didn't mention; label as additional example.\n\nNeed mention relevant dependencies and counterfactuals. EDT's counterfactuals? It doesn't use intervention; it uses conditional probability P(O|A) as ordinary Bayesian conditional. But if A is act chosen by agent, conditioning may incorporate agent's beliefs about own decision algorithm. There are problems with conditionalizing on one's own action if the action is known to be chosen by a rational procedure. Need perhaps discuss \"why ain'cha rich?\" and ratifiability. Need maybe discuss decision-making under EDT: To decide unfamiliar case: specify act partition, outcome partition, assign joint probabilities over acts/outcomes (or states generating outcomes and correlations), assign utilities, compute conditional expected utility for each act. Choose max. If policies, enumerate policies and compute V(π)=E[D(O)|π]. Need maybe mention expected utility with states: V(A)=Σ_s P(S=s | A) U(A,s) if outcome determined by action and state. But conditioning on A may change distribution over states due to correlation. Need emphasize EDT does not require causal graph but user can use one to compute conditionals; dependencies relevant if P(S|A)≠P(S). It includes causal effects (if A causes O) and noncausal correlations (if common cause, prediction, logical correlation). If action is probabilistically independent of outcomes, EDT equals outcome prior expected utility maybe.\n\nNeed address open question: how to set conditional probabilities for actions, especially when agent's choice is under deliberation? Source leaves it open. Operational interpretation: use agent's full probabilistic model including her decision procedure and any predictors/correlations, then condition on the proposition \"I perform A\". If multiple possible decision algorithms, condition on event. But self-undermining: P(A) can be influenced by EDT itself. Maybe use \"deliberation dynamics\" or \"ratifiability\": an EDT choice is admissible if it maximizes conditional expected utility given that the agent is choosing according to EDT? Need maybe propose: in unfamiliar cases, build a causal/graphical model, then compute evidential conditionals by Bayesian conditioning on observed action variable, not intervening. If there is a causal graph, P(O|do(A)) for CDT; P(O|A) for EDT. For zero-probability actions, use limits, conditional chance functions, or assume small tremble probabilities (epsilon exploration) to make conditioning well-defined. That's an operational interpretation we can justify.\n\nNeed discuss policies: EDT often evaluates acts one at a time, but can evaluate policies by conditioning on policy adoption. In sequential decisions, if future self re-decides with EDT, may create dynamic inconsistency. Example? Maybe Newcomb with precommitment? EDT may prefer precommitment? Need not go too deep. But user asks evaluates alternative actions or policies and how treats relevant dependencies and counterfactuals. We can discuss policies as acts: set of contingent instructions. Evaluate by conditional probability of outcomes given that policy is implemented. If policy includes future choices, condition on the whole pattern. But if the agent at later point makes a new EDT evaluation, she might choose differently; hence need distinguish evaluating a policy ex ante vs act-by-act. EDT source doesn't fully resolve; propose evaluating stable policies under assumption agent follows EDT or using ratifiable policies. Need mention superrationality? Source mentions superrationality. Twin PD: perfect correlation. EDT acts as if one's choice is evidence of twin's choice. But if correlation imperfect, compute with conditional probs.\n\nNeed philosophical motivation: \"manage the news\", choose action that is best news. It can be understood as choosing the action whose occurrence would maximize expected desirability in one's posterior. It is not about causing, but about what the action indicates. Lewis criticism: irrational policy of managing news; Joyce: causal efficacy not auspiciousness. We can explain: EDT may recommend choosing an action because it is a symptom of good outcome, even if it doesn't cause it. This is controversial. But in cases with predictors/logical correlations, the action is not merely symptom? The source says EDT one-boxes Newcomb and cooperates in twin PD. Need explain.\n\nNeed maybe include mathematical details: probability space (Ω, F, P), utility function U: outcomes→ℝ. Act partition A_i mutually exclusive exhaustive. Outcome partition O_j. Desirability D(O_j). EDT value:\nV(A_i) = Σ_j P(O_j | A_i) D(O_j) = E_P[D | A_i].\nChoice rule: choose A* ∈ argmax_i V(A_i). If continuous: V(A)=∫ D(o) dP(o|A). If states: V(A)=Σ_s P(S=s | A) U(A,S). Note D(O_j) maybe outcome utility independent of act; if utilities depend on act too, use utility over (A,S) or outcome. Need mention conditional probability P(O_j|A_i) = P(O_j∧A_i)/P(A_i). If P(A_i)=0, problem. Could define conditional probability as primitive or use regular conditional distribution. In decision contexts, assign small positive probability to all possible acts (trembling hand) or specify conditional chances directly.\n\nNeed distinguish conditional probability vs intervention: in a causal Bayes net, EDT computes observational conditional; CDT computes interventional P(O|do(A)). Example with common cause: lesion. Suppose lesion L causes both desire to smoke S and cancer C. P(C|S)>P(C), so EDT value of smoking lower if cancer undesirable; but do(S) doesn't change C. That shows EDT can be \"evidential\" too much. However EDT defenders may respond by distinguishing that if smoking is chosen by EDT? Maybe if lesion causes decision to smoke, conditioning on smoking still evidence. It recommends not smoking if you'd rather not have cancer? Actually if smoking gives pleasure, tradeoff. If no pleasure and smoking evidence of cancer, EDT says don't smoke because learning you smoke is bad news. CDT says indifferent or smoke if pleasure. This illustrates criticism. Need label as my addition.\n\nNeed maybe discuss Newcomb formal: predictor accuracy p=0.99. Need compute carefully. Let p = P(predict one-box | one-box) = P(predict two-box | two-box) = 0.99? The source uses P(1M|one-box)=0.99, P(1M|two-box)=0.01. That assumes predictor symmetric and prior? Actually if predictor 99% accurate conditional on choice? Need note. In Newcomb, if predictor reliability given by conditional probabilities, EDT values as source. More generally with predictor accuracy p and prior q? We can derive:\nLet M=box B contains $1M. If predictor perfect correlation: P(M|one-box)=p, P(M|two-box)=1-p (assuming symmetric). Then V1=p*1,000,000; V2=(1-p)*1,001,000 + p*1,000? Wait if p probability predictor predicts one-box given one-box, then if two-box, probability predictor predicted one-box is 1-p? Yes if symmetric. Then V1=p*1M, V2=(1-p)*(1,001,000)+p*1,000. With p=.99 gives V1=990k, V2=0.01*1,001,000 + .99*1,000 = 10,010+990=11,000. Source. Could mention if accuracy p> threshold: one-box if pM > (1-p)(M+1000)+p*1000 = (1-p)M+1000? Actually V2=(1-p)(M+1000)+p*1000 = (1-p)M+1000. One-box better if pM > (1-p)M + 1000 => (2p-1)M > 1000 => p > 0.5 + 1000/(2M) ≈ 0.5005 for M=1M. So even barely better than chance. Good mathematical insight. But careful if predictor not symmetric; could mention.\n\nTwin PD: source says exact psychological twin, probabilities 1. If imperfect correlation q = P(twin C | self C) = P(twin D | self D) maybe. Compute V(C)=q*5+(1-q)*0? Wait if self cooperates, twin cooperates with q, if twin defects self gets 0 (if one cooperates other defects cooperator gets 0). V(D)= (1-q)*10 + q*1? If q probability twin matches? Let q=P(twin C|self C)=P(twin D|self D). Then V(C)=q*5+(1-q)*0=5q. V(D)=P(twin C|D)*10 + P(twin D|D)*1 = (1-q)*10 + q*1 =10-9q. Cooperate if 5q > 10-9q =>14q>10 => q>5/7≈0.714. In perfect q=1. Good extension. Could use to show unfamiliar cases. Need ensure payoffs: if one cooperates one defects, defector gets 10, cooperator 0. Yes.\n\nNeed discuss Sleeping Beauty briefly? Source mentions. Could avoid too deep; perhaps mention as unresolved application. But user likely wants central definitions and reasoning. We can mention source says EDT applied to Sleeping Beauty to avoid Dutch books, but disputed by Conitzer. Need maybe explain not fully resolved. We can state: source leaves exact credence/action link open; EDT alone is decision rule, not full theory of self-locating belief; to use it in Sleeping Beauty one must choose credence update rule. If Beauty's bets are treated as repeated choices conditioned on awakening, EDT with thirder probabilities may avoid some Dutch books, but halfers may still be vulnerable according to Conitzer. We can avoid detailed calculations due length. But maybe include short paragraph.\n\nNeed target 2400-3000 words. Need count roughly. Need maybe around 2600 words. Final essay with headings. Need include references maybe minimal. Use source and key refs from source. Need no appendices. Need make clear source vs own additions. Could say: The following follows the Wikipedia article for core definition and examples; anything beyond (states, zero probability, causal-net operationalization, threshold examples, policy ratifiability) is my elaboration. Need maybe in text: \"The source states...\" \"I add...\".\n\nNeed structure:\nTitle: Evidential Decision Theory: Choosing the Best News\n1. Introduction: decision theory, EDT motto.\n2. Formal setup: acts, outcomes, probability, utility, formula. Policy version. Compare with CDT formula.\n3. How to apply: step-by-step: define act partition, outcome partition, assign joint distribution or state model, compute conditionals, maximize. Dependencies: any statistical dependency matters. Causal graph observational vs interventional. Zero probability and self-referential choices open; propose operational interpretation.\n4. Examples: Newcomb (with threshold), Twin PD (with imperfect correlation), Smoking lesion (my example) to illustrate criticism and dependency treatment.\n5. Policies, counterfactuals, dynamic choice: evaluate policies as events; EDT's counterfactuals are evidential conditionals; if policy affects predictor beliefs etc. Ratifiability / stable deliberation. Uncertainty.\n6. Philosophical motivation and criticisms: news management vs causal efficacy; EDT can be prudentially rational if correlations real; source's Evidentialist's Wager; Lewis/Joyce criticism.\n7. Conclusion.\nReferences: maybe 4-6. Need include Wikipedia revision, Gibbard & Harper, Ahmed, Lewis, Joyce maybe MacAskill. Keep brief.\n\nNeed ensure final is an essay not answer to assigned case. Need no direct instruction mention. Need likely ~2700 words. Let's draft with enough detail.\n\nNeed think about exact mathematical rigor.\n\nFormal setup:\nLet \\mathcal A={A_1,...,A_n} acts. Let \\mathcal O={O_1,...,O_m} outcomes. Agent has probability measure P over algebra containing acts and outcomes. Desirability D: \\mathcal O→ℝ. EDT value:\nV(A_i)=E_P[D(O) | A_i] = Σ_j D(O_j) P(O_j | A_i)\nprovided P(A_i)>0. Choice: A_i ≽_EDT A_k iff V(A_i)≥V(A_k). If ties, arbitrary. If acts not exhaustive? Use partition.\nIf outcomes determined by states S: O_j = f(A_i,S). Then V(A_i)=Σ_s P(S=s | A_i) U(A_i,s). The conditional P(S|A) can differ from P(S). EDT's central move is to let choice update beliefs about states via evidential dependence. In standard Savage causal expected utility, you might use P(S) independent of acts (states are probabilistically independent). EDT rejects requiring act-state independence; acts can be evidence of states.\n\nBut in decision theory, one worry: if agent can choose freely, should P(A) be positive? If agent's deliberation determines A, conditioning on A might be weird because she knows she will choose what she judges best. This can lead to Egan-style cases? Maybe not necessary. But mention zero probability.\n\nCDT formula: U(A)=Σ_j P(A □→ O_j)D(O_j). In causal Bayes net, U(A)=Σ_j P(O_j | do(A))D(O_j). If A is causally independent of O but correlated via common cause, P(O|A)≠P(O|do(A)). EDT uses observation, CDT uses intervention.\n\nCounterfactuals: EDT's counterfactual? It can be expressed as \"if I were to learn that A was chosen, what would my expected utility be?\" This is not necessarily closest-world intervention. If action probability zero, one can interpret via conditional desirability: Jeffrey's desirability V(A)=Des(A∧O)? Need maybe not go deep.\n\nPolicy evaluation:\nA policy π maps histories to actions. Let [π] be proposition \"agent implements π\". Then V(π)=E[D(O)|π]. If policy includes future actions, condition on all those actions. If policies are correlated with states/predictors, same evidential logic. In game theory, if multiple agents use similar algorithms, conditioning on own policy can be evidence of others' policies (superrationality). Source mentions twin PD and supporting argument.\n\nImportant open questions:\n1. Which probabilities? EDT needs a joint distribution including one's own action. In self-referential/predictor cases, how to set? Operational proposal: model decision algorithm as a variable D, predictor P, state S. Assign prior based on empirical/logical uncertainty. Then compute observational conditionals. If exact deterministic algorithm makes P(A) 0 or 1, add noise/trembles or condition on soft evidence. Justification: real agents are never logically omniscient and predictors are imperfect; limits recover ideal cases.\n2. Ratifiability: If an EDT agent conditions on the fact that she is choosing EDT, some choices may change value. A choice is ratifiable if it maximizes V conditional on the agent using EDT (or on the chosen decision rule). This can resolve some unstable cases; I propose using ratifiable EDT choices when simple maximization is self-undermining. Need mention source does not discuss.\n3. Dynamic inconsistency: Ex ante policy vs act-by-act. EDT may endorse precommitment to policies that later seem suboptimal? Or vice versa. Need propose: For binding policies, evaluate V(π) ex ante; for nonbinding sequential choice, evaluate each choice with updated conditional; if conflict, specify whether agent can commit.\n\nExamples in detail:\nNewcomb:\nLet T = take only B, B2 = take both. Predictor accuracy p. Let M=box B has million. Source assumes P(M|T)=p, P(M|B2)=1-p. Utilities money linear. V(T)=p*1,000,000. V(B2)=(1-p)*1,001,000 + p*1,000. For p=.99: 990k vs 11k. EDT one-box. Threshold p>0.5005. Note CDT: since prediction already made, P(M □→ T?) Actually two-boxing causally adds $1k regardless of box B, so CDT two-boxes. This illustrates EDT counts predictor correlation as reason.\n\nNeed maybe mention if predictor perfect: V(T)=1M, V(B2)=1k.\n\nTwin PD:\nPerfect twin: V(C)=5, V(D)=1. Cooperate. Imperfect correlation q threshold 5/7. Shows how to apply when dependency less than perfect. If correlation due to independent random decisions q=0.5? Then V(C)=2.5, V(D)=5.5? Actually q=.5: V(C)=2.5; V(D)=10-4.5=5.5, defect. EDT only cooperates if high enough correlation. This helps unfamiliar cases. Need distinguish source assumes exactly analogous reasoning gives probability 1. But what if not perfect? My extension.\n\nSmoking lesion (added):\nL lesion causes both smoking and cancer, no causal link smoke->cancer. Suppose no pleasure. P(C|S)=0.9, P(C|¬S)=0.1, cancer utility -100, no cancer 0. V(S)=0.9(-100)+0.1(0)=-90; V(¬S)=0.1(-100)+0.9(0)=-10. EDT says don't smoke, even though smoking does not cause cancer. CDT with do(S) gives same cancer rates, so indifferent or if pleasure positive smoke. This is the classic criticism: EDT may avoid bad news by refraining from symptoms. But in Newcomb/twin cases, one might argue dependencies are not mere symptoms? EDT doesn't internally distinguish unless supplemented with causal info. This is important.\n\nSleeping Beauty:\nMaybe a paragraph. Need not too long. Source says EDT applied to avoid Dutch books; halfers perhaps still vulnerable. I can state: EDT is not by itself a theory of indexical credence; to apply, one must specify probability of awakening/coin. If Beauty makes bets, EDT evaluates bet offers conditional on awakening; different credences (1/2 vs 1/3) lead to different verdicts. Source notes debate. This shows open boundary.\n\nPhilosophical:\nEDT as \"do what you most want to learn that you will do\". It treats choice as evidence. In a world with predictors, copies, correlated algorithms, choosing an act can be evidence of many similar choices; stakes higher (Evidentialist's Wager). But Lewis says managing news not world-making. The central dispute: whether rational choice should track causal production or information. EDT's answer: if you care about outcomes, and action is reliable evidence of outcome, conditional expected utility measures the value of the news. Critics reply: you can't eat news; you should cause good outcomes.\n\nNeed maybe mention expected utility axioms? Not necessary. But could say EDT is not an alternative to expected utility but a specification of probabilities.\n\nNeed ensure word count. Let's estimate. If final with headings maybe ~2600. Need maybe not too many references. But references count in words. Could be 2500.\n\nNeed produce final answer only. Need maybe include equations in LaTeX. Ensure readability.\n\nPotential pitfalls: Source says formula with P(O_j | A) D(O_j), D desirability. Need not confuse with utility of actions. Need maybe mention if outcome utilities include action itself, formula can be generalized.\n\nNeed discuss \"counterfactuals\" more explicitly: EDT conditionals are material conditional conditioning? There is a distinction: P(O|A) vs P(A □→ O). In Gibbard/Harper, CDT uses counterfactual probabilities. EDT uses conditional probabilities. But what about if action is known not to occur? Counterfactual conditional might differ. EDT's \"if I were to do A\" can be understood as conditional on the proposition A, not intervention. However if A has probability zero due to deterministic intention, ordinary conditioning fails. Need propose operational interpretation: use Jeffrey's radical probabilism? Maybe \"conditional desirability\" defined directly. For practical use, assign small positive probability to all acts (epsilon randomization), compute, then take limit. This is reasonable. Need include.\n\nNeed maybe mention dominance: EDT can violate causal dominance: Newcomb two-boxing dominates one-boxing causally? In Newcomb, if B fixed, two-box yields $1k more than one-box in each state (predicted one-box: two-box 1,001,000 vs one-box 1,000,000; predicted two-box: two-box 1,000 vs one-box 0). Yet EDT one-boxes. This highlights. Could include.\n\nNeed perhaps discuss policies in Newcomb: EDT might choose policy \"one-box\" before prediction? If predictor predicts policy. Same result. If agent can precommit to two-box after? Predictor may predict. EDT evaluates precommitment by conditional outcome. If precommitment changes predictor, etc.\n\nNeed maybe include \"relevant dependencies\" definition: dependency is relevant if it survives conditioning on all available information. That is, use posterior probability after evidence, but before action. If a dependency is explained away by a common cause? Need maybe: compute conditional probabilities relative to agent's total evidence E: V_E(A)=Σ P(O_j | A, E)D(O_j). This is important. In real cases, include all evidence. For example, if you know you have lesion, smoking no longer evidence of cancer? Actually if know lesion, P(C|S,L)=P(C|L) maybe; EDT would not avoid smoking due to cancer. So EDT's verdict depends on what is conditioned on. Need include. That helps operational. Formula with total evidence E:\nV(A)=Σ_j P(O_j | A, E) D(O_j). The source omits E but assumed. Need mention as my elaboration/standard. This is important for unfamiliar cases: include all background evidence, then condition on action. If you know common cause, correlation may vanish.\n\nNeed also discuss acts as propositions. Need maybe avoid too formal.\n\nNeed include references: Wikipedia, Gibbard & Harper, Ahmed, Lewis, Joyce, MacAskill et al. Maybe Conitzer. But avoid lengthy bibliography. Maybe list 6 references.\n\nNeed ensure not plagiarize source but paraphrase. Use own account.\n\nLet's start drafting final.\n\nPossible title: \"Evidential Decision Theory: Expected Utility as News\".\n\nOpening: \"Evidential decision theory (EDT) is a version of expected-utility theory in which the value of an act is its conditional expected desirability...\" quote source. Then mention source.\n\nNeed maybe state \"The core definition and examples below follow the Wikipedia revision; extensions are marked.\" Good.\n\nDetailed draft:\n\n---\n\nEvidential decision theory...\n\n1. Core idea\n\nWhen agent receives news \"you did A\", how good would world seem? EDT says choose act maximizing that posterior expected utility. Motto from Ahmed via Wikipedia: do what you most want to learn you will do. It contrasts with CDT... etc.\n\n2. Formal definition\n\nProbability space etc. Include total evidence E.\n\nLet acts A_i partition, outcomes O_j. Agent's probability P(·|E), desirability D. Define:\nV_E(A_i)=Σ_j P(O_j | A_i, E) D(O_j) = E[D|A_i,E]. If P(A_i|E)=0, conditional undefined; see §4.\n\nChoice rule: choose argmax. If continuous integrals. State form: V(A)=Σ_s P(S=s|A,E) U(A,s). Note states not independent.\n\nCDT formula: U(A)=Σ_j P(A □→ O_j | E) D(O_j), or do. Difference: observational vs interventional.\n\n3. Dependencies and counterfactuals\n\nEDT counts any probabilistic dependence given evidence: causal, predictive, common cause, logical/correlated reasoning. If A and O share common cause, conditionals track symptom. It treats \"counterfactual\" as conditionalization: hypothetical supposition A. In causal Bayes net, EDT is P(O|A,E), CDT is P(O|do(A),E). This makes clear.\n\nExample smoking lesion. Include math. Show EDT may choose symptom management. This is source not mention; my addition. This also illustrates why critics object. But EDT can respond? Maybe if action is caused by lesion, \"not smoking\" may also be evidence? Actually if lesion causes smoking, not smoking evidence no lesion. EDT values no smoking. If you know lesion status, correlation disappears. Include.\n\n4. Open operational questions\n\n- Probabilities over own actions. Need assign joint P(A,O). Since action is chosen, not observed randomly. Source leaves this. Proposal: model agent's decision process and predictors as variables; assign probabilities to possible choices as uncertain events from deliberative perspective. Use small trembles for zero-prob acts. Or conditional probabilities primitive.\n- Total evidence and self-information. Conditioning on \"I am an EDT agent choosing A\" can change values. Ratifiability: choose A only if V(A | I am using EDT and choose A?) Hmm. Need formulate carefully.\nMaybe: Let R be proposition \"I am reasoning according to EDT\". A ratifiable EDT choice satisfies A∈argmax V(A' | R,E). In some problems, unqualified EDT may choose an act that, once recognized as chosen by EDT, loses value; ratifiability filters. This is my proposal. Need not overcomplicate.\n- Policies. Policy π evaluated by V(π)=E[D|π,E]. For sequential: if policy binding, evaluate ex ante; if not, each choice re-evaluated; dynamic inconsistency possible. Reasonable operational interpretation: evaluate the object actually under agent's control (one-shot act vs commitment policy). If deciding policy, condition on policy adoption; if deciding immediate act, condition on act. Source doesn't resolve.\n\n5. Examples\n\nNewcomb. Full. Need maybe use p general. Compute threshold. Include CDT reasoning.\n\nTwin prisoner's dilemma. Full and q threshold.\n\nMaybe Sleeping Beauty short.\n\n6. Philosophical stakes\n\nLewis/Joyce criticisms, supporting arguments MacAskill. EDT as managing news vs causing. In correlated world, news may be causally irrelevant but decision may coordinate with copies. Need mention \"Evidentialist's Wager\" source: even if less credence, EDT may have higher stakes because correlated decision-makers. But be cautious: source says may be reasonable to act as if EDT true because stakes higher. We can summarize.\n\n7. Conclusion.\n\nReferences.\n\nNeed word count. Let's write with maybe 2500-2800. Need not mention word count. Ensure no weird markdown issues. Use LaTeX. Need maybe include equations with align.\n\nLet's draft in final. Need keep precise. Let's count rough as I write. Maybe final about 2700 words.\n\nDetailed content:\n\nTitle and intro 200 words.\nFormal definition 600.\nDependencies 500.\nOperational 500.\nExamples 800.\nPhilosophy 300.\nConclusion/references 300. Total 3200? Need keep concise. Target 2400-3000. Need maybe 2700.\n\nLet's draft carefully.\n\nPotential issue: In Newcomb threshold formula, if predictor accuracy p = P(predict one-box | one-box) = P(predict two-box | two-box). Then V(one)=p*1M + (1-p)*0? Wait if predictor predicts one-box then B contains M. If you one-box but predictor predicted two-box (prob 1-p), B empty and you get 0. Yes V1=p*1M. If two-box: if predictor predicted one-box (prob? P(pred one|two)=1-p if symmetric), B contains M and you get M+1000. If predicted two (prob p), B empty and get 1000. So V2=(1-p)(M+1000)+p*1000 = (1-p)M+1000. Good. Threshold pM > (1-p)M+1000 => (2p-1)M > 1000. With M=1,000,000, p > .5005. Good. Source uses p=.99.\n\nTwin PD q threshold: Define q=P(twin cooperates | I cooperate)=P(twin defects | I defect) (symmetry). Then V(C)=5q +0(1-q)=5q. V(D)=10(1-q)+1q = 10-9q. C if q>10/14=5/7. If perfect q=1, C. If q=0.5, D. Good.\n\nSmoking lesion math: Let C cancer, S smoke. Utilities: U(no cancer)=0, U(cancer)=-100; smoking no direct pleasure. Suppose P(C|S)=0.9, P(C|¬S)=0.1. V(S)=-90, V(¬S)=-10. EDT no smoke. If direct pleasure +20, V(S)=-70, still no smoke; if +100 maybe indifferent. CDT: if do(S) doesn't change cancer, V do smoke = direct pleasure + expected cancer based on lesion? Need not compute.\n\nSleeping Beauty: brief. Could say source states EDT used to avoid Dutch books but Conitzer argues halfers still vulnerable. EDT alone doesn't settle. Need maybe not too detailed.\n\nPolicy and counterfactual section maybe after examples? Could do before examples? Let's structure:\n1 Core idea\n2 Formal model\n3 How EDT treats dependencies and counterfactuals\n4 Applying EDT: examples (Newcomb, twin, lesion)\n5 Policies, zero probability, and ratifiability (open questions)\n6 Philosophical implications and criticism\nConclusion\nReferences\n\nMaybe put zero probability earlier? It's okay.\n\nNeed mention source claims vs own: use phrases: \"The source gives...\" \"I will add...\" \"This example is not in the source...\".\n\nNeed ensure final not too answer-like. It should be essay. Good.\n\nLet's draft final now. Need include references with source URL and permanent revision. Maybe references at end:\n1. Wikipedia contributors, \"Evidential decision theory,\" revision 1373679688, 2026.\n2. Gibbard & Harper 1976.\n3. Ahmed 2021.\n4. Lewis 1981.\n5. Joyce 1999.\n6. MacAskill et al. 2021.\nMaybe Conitzer 2015.\nNeed avoid lengthy bibliographies but okay 6.\n\nNeed ensure word count maybe 2600. Let's write.\n\nPotential additions: \"news value\" definition: The news value of A is expected utility after conditionalizing on A. If D is desirability of propositions, Jeffrey's formula maybe V(A)=Σ P(O_j|A)D(O_j). Good.\n\nNeed mention if outcomes not mutually exclusive? Use partition.\n\nNeed include \"conditional on total evidence E\" formula. Source doesn't but standard. Need mark as elaboration.\n\nNeed discuss \"counterfactual\" exactly: EDT's P(O|A) can be understood as probability of O under the supposition A. It may not match probability in closest possible world where A is brought about by an intervention. For deterministic cases, conditional probability may be unstable; use limits.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"why EDT one-boxes even though two-boxing dominates\": In each predictor state, two-box yields more, but EDT conditions choice on predictor state. Good.\n\nLet's begin final. Ensure no excessive repetition. Use headings. Need maybe around 2700 words. Let's write in polished style.\n\nCheck final for any instructions not to include? User wants finish brief conclusion. Good.\n\nPotential problem: Source date 2026? It says revision 2026-09-07. Fine. We can cite.\n\nLet's produce final.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need answer user's request: write a mathematical essay/blog post explaining EDT and applying to decision problems. Target 2400-3000 words including references. Need coherent, precise enough for reader to decide unfamiliar cases. Explain how theory evaluates actions/policies, dependencies, counterfactuals. Where source leaves important question open, explain uncertainty and propose operational interpretation if reasonable. Distinguish source claims vs own assumptions. Use examples. Avoid unnecessary repetition, lengthy bibliographies, appendices. Finish brief conclusion. Source is Wikipedia article on Evidential decision theory. Need likely cite source and maybe references from it. Need produce final essay.\n\nNeed think deeply about EDT. Need define formal decision theory: states, actions, outcomes, utility/desirability, probability function. EDT value V(A)=sum_j P(O_j|A)D(O_j). Choose action maximizing V. News value: do what you most want to learn you will do. Contrast CDT U(A)=sum P(A □→ O_j)D(O_j). Need discuss conditional probabilities vs counterfactuals, dependency. EDT treats all probabilistic dependencies as decision-relevant, whether causal, common-cause, logical/evidential, predictor correlations. It conditions on the action as evidence. But action may be impossible/probability zero; need regularity or conditional probabilities as primitive / Jeffrey conditioning? Need mention acts with zero prior probability and conditional desirability maybe. Need policies: if policy is chosen, condition on event \"agent adopts/executes policy π\". For dynamic problems, EDT can evaluate policies by conditioning on entire policy; may face dynamic inconsistency if later choices re-evaluate? Need discuss. Need apply to Newcomb, twin prisoner's dilemma, maybe smoking lesion? Source doesn't mention smoking lesion but common criticism. Could include as own example? It would help explain dependency vs counterfactual. But need be careful: user wants use examples whenever helpful. We can include smoking lesion to show EDT's potential pathology: lesion causes both smoking and cancer, smoking evidence of cancer but doesn't cause it; EDT might avoid smoking if cancer bad, CDT doesn't. This illustrates dependency treatment and open questions about spurious correlation. But source didn't mention; label as additional example.\n\nNeed mention relevant dependencies and counterfactuals. EDT's counterfactuals? It doesn't use intervention; it uses conditional probability P(O|A) as ordinary Bayesian conditional. But if A is act chosen by agent, conditioning may incorporate agent's beliefs about own decision algorithm. There are problems with conditionalizing on one's own action if the action is known to be chosen by a rational procedure. Need perhaps discuss \"why ain'cha rich?\" and ratifiability. Need maybe discuss decision-making under EDT: To decide unfamiliar case: specify act partition, outcome partition, assign joint probabilities over acts/outcomes (or states generating outcomes and correlations), assign utilities, compute conditional expected utility for each act. Choose max. If policies, enumerate policies and compute V(π)=E[D(O)|π]. Need maybe mention expected utility with states: V(A)=Σ_s P(S=s | A) U(A,s) if outcome determined by action and state. But conditioning on A may change distribution over states due to correlation. Need emphasize EDT does not require causal graph but user can use one to compute conditionals; dependencies relevant if P(S|A)≠P(S). It includes causal effects (if A causes O) and noncausal correlations (if common cause, prediction, logical correlation). If action is probabilistically independent of outcomes, EDT equals outcome prior expected utility maybe.\n\nNeed address open question: how to set conditional probabilities for actions, especially when agent's choice is under deliberation? Source leaves it open. Operational interpretation: use agent's full probabilistic model including her decision procedure and any predictors/correlations, then condition on the proposition \"I perform A\". If multiple possible decision algorithms, condition on event. But self-undermining: P(A) can be influenced by EDT itself. Maybe use \"deliberation dynamics\" or \"ratifiability\": an EDT choice is admissible if it maximizes conditional expected utility given that the agent is choosing according to EDT? Need maybe propose: in unfamiliar cases, build a causal/graphical model, then compute evidential conditionals by Bayesian conditioning on observed action variable, not intervening. If there is a causal graph, P(O|do(A)) for CDT; P(O|A) for EDT. For zero-probability actions, use limits, conditional chance functions, or assume small tremble probabilities (epsilon exploration) to make conditioning well-defined. That's an operational interpretation we can justify.\n\nNeed discuss policies: EDT often evaluates acts one at a time, but can evaluate policies by conditioning on policy adoption. In sequential decisions, if future self re-decides with EDT, may create dynamic inconsistency. Example? Maybe Newcomb with precommitment? EDT may prefer precommitment? Need not go too deep. But user asks evaluates alternative actions or policies and how treats relevant dependencies and counterfactuals. We can discuss policies as acts: set of contingent instructions. Evaluate by conditional probability of outcomes given that policy is implemented. If policy includes future choices, condition on the whole pattern. But if the agent at later point makes a new EDT evaluation, she might choose differently; hence need distinguish evaluating a policy ex ante vs act-by-act. EDT source doesn't fully resolve; propose evaluating stable policies under assumption agent follows EDT or using ratifiable policies. Need mention superrationality? Source mentions superrationality. Twin PD: perfect correlation. EDT acts as if one's choice is evidence of twin's choice. But if correlation imperfect, compute with conditional probs.\n\nNeed philosophical motivation: \"manage the news\", choose action that is best news. It can be understood as choosing the action whose occurrence would maximize expected desirability in one's posterior. It is not about causing, but about what the action indicates. Lewis criticism: irrational policy of managing news; Joyce: causal efficacy not auspiciousness. We can explain: EDT may recommend choosing an action because it is a symptom of good outcome, even if it doesn't cause it. This is controversial. But in cases with predictors/logical correlations, the action is not merely symptom? The source says EDT one-boxes Newcomb and cooperates in twin PD. Need explain.\n\nNeed maybe include mathematical details: probability space (Ω, F, P), utility function U: outcomes→ℝ. Act partition A_i mutually exclusive exhaustive. Outcome partition O_j. Desirability D(O_j). EDT value:\nV(A_i) = Σ_j P(O_j | A_i) D(O_j) = E_P[D | A_i].\nChoice rule: choose A* ∈ argmax_i V(A_i). If continuous: V(A)=∫ D(o) dP(o|A). If states: V(A)=Σ_s P(S=s | A) U(A,S). Note D(O_j) maybe outcome utility independent of act; if utilities depend on act too, use utility over (A,S) or outcome. Need mention conditional probability P(O_j|A_i) = P(O_j∧A_i)/P(A_i). If P(A_i)=0, problem. Could define conditional probability as primitive or use regular conditional distribution. In decision contexts, assign small positive probability to all possible acts (trembling hand) or specify conditional chances directly.\n\nNeed distinguish conditional probability vs intervention: in a causal Bayes net, EDT computes observational conditional; CDT computes interventional P(O|do(A)). Example with common cause: lesion. Suppose lesion L causes both desire to smoke S and cancer C. P(C|S)>P(C), so EDT value of smoking lower if cancer undesirable; but do(S) doesn't change C. That shows EDT can be \"evidential\" too much. However EDT defenders may respond by distinguishing that if smoking is chosen by EDT? Maybe if lesion causes decision to smoke, conditioning on smoking still evidence. It recommends not smoking if you'd rather not have cancer? Actually if smoking gives pleasure, tradeoff. If no pleasure and smoking evidence of cancer, EDT says don't smoke because learning you smoke is bad news. CDT says indifferent or smoke if pleasure. This illustrates criticism. Need label as my addition.\n\nNeed maybe discuss Newcomb formal: predictor accuracy p=0.99. Need compute carefully. Let p = P(predict one-box | one-box) = P(predict two-box | two-box) = 0.99? The source uses P(1M|one-box)=0.99, P(1M|two-box)=0.01. That assumes predictor symmetric and prior? Actually if predictor 99% accurate conditional on choice? Need note. In Newcomb, if predictor reliability given by conditional probabilities, EDT values as source. More generally with predictor accuracy p and prior q? We can derive:\nLet M=box B contains $1M. If predictor perfect correlation: P(M|one-box)=p, P(M|two-box)=1-p (assuming symmetric). Then V1=p*1,000,000; V2=(1-p)*1,001,000 + p*1,000? Wait if p probability predictor predicts one-box given one-box, then if two-box, probability predictor predicted one-box is 1-p? Yes if symmetric. Then V1=p*1M, V2=(1-p)*(1,001,000)+p*1,000. With p=.99 gives V1=990k, V2=0.01*1,001,000 + .99*1,000 = 10,010+990=11,000. Source. Could mention if accuracy p> threshold: one-box if pM > (1-p)(M+1000)+p*1000 = (1-p)M+1000? Actually V2=(1-p)(M+1000)+p*1000 = (1-p)M+1000. One-box better if pM > (1-p)M + 1000 => (2p-1)M > 1000 => p > 0.5 + 1000/(2M) ≈ 0.5005 for M=1M. So even barely better than chance. Good mathematical insight. But careful if predictor not symmetric; could mention.\n\nTwin PD: source says exact psychological twin, probabilities 1. If imperfect correlation q = P(twin C | self C) = P(twin D | self D) maybe. Compute V(C)=q*5+(1-q)*0? Wait if self cooperates, twin cooperates with q, if twin defects self gets 0 (if one cooperates other defects cooperator gets 0). V(D)= (1-q)*10 + q*1? If q probability twin matches? Let q=P(twin C|self C)=P(twin D|self D). Then V(C)=q*5+(1-q)*0=5q. V(D)=P(twin C|D)*10 + P(twin D|D)*1 = (1-q)*10 + q*1 =10-9q. Cooperate if 5q > 10-9q =>14q>10 => q>5/7≈0.714. In perfect q=1. Good extension. Could use to show unfamiliar cases. Need ensure payoffs: if one cooperates one defects, defector gets 10, cooperator 0. Yes.\n\nNeed discuss Sleeping Beauty briefly? Source mentions. Could avoid too deep; perhaps mention as unresolved application. But user likely wants central definitions and reasoning. We can mention source says EDT applied to Sleeping Beauty to avoid Dutch books, but disputed by Conitzer. Need maybe explain not fully resolved. We can state: source leaves exact credence/action link open; EDT alone is decision rule, not full theory of self-locating belief; to use it in Sleeping Beauty one must choose credence update rule. If Beauty's bets are treated as repeated choices conditioned on awakening, EDT with thirder probabilities may avoid some Dutch books, but halfers may still be vulnerable according to Conitzer. We can avoid detailed calculations due length. But maybe include short paragraph.\n\nNeed target 2400-3000 words. Need count roughly. Need maybe around 2600 words. Final essay with headings. Need include references maybe minimal. Use source and key refs from source. Need no appendices. Need make clear source vs own additions. Could say: The following follows the Wikipedia article for core definition and examples; anything beyond (states, zero probability, causal-net operationalization, threshold examples, policy ratifiability) is my elaboration. Need maybe in text: \"The source states...\" \"I add...\".\n\nNeed structure:\nTitle: Evidential Decision Theory: Choosing the Best News\n1. Introduction: decision theory, EDT motto.\n2. Formal setup: acts, outcomes, probability, utility, formula. Policy version. Compare with CDT formula.\n3. How to apply: step-by-step: define act partition, outcome partition, assign joint distribution or state model, compute conditionals, maximize. Dependencies: any statistical dependency matters. Causal graph observational vs interventional. Zero probability and self-referential choices open; propose operational interpretation.\n4. Examples: Newcomb (with threshold), Twin PD (with imperfect correlation), Smoking lesion (my example) to illustrate criticism and dependency treatment.\n5. Policies, counterfactuals, dynamic choice: evaluate policies as events; EDT's counterfactuals are evidential conditionals; if policy affects predictor beliefs etc. Ratifiability / stable deliberation. Uncertainty.\n6. Philosophical motivation and criticisms: news management vs causal efficacy; EDT can be prudentially rational if correlations real; source's Evidentialist's Wager; Lewis/Joyce criticism.\n7. Conclusion.\nReferences: maybe 4-6. Need include Wikipedia revision, Gibbard & Harper, Ahmed, Lewis, Joyce maybe MacAskill. Keep brief.\n\nNeed ensure final is an essay not answer to assigned case. Need no direct instruction mention. Need likely ~2700 words. Let's draft with enough detail.\n\nNeed think about exact mathematical rigor.\n\nFormal setup:\nLet \\mathcal A={A_1,...,A_n} acts. Let \\mathcal O={O_1,...,O_m} outcomes. Agent has probability measure P over algebra containing acts and outcomes. Desirability D: \\mathcal O→ℝ. EDT value:\nV(A_i)=E_P[D(O) | A_i] = Σ_j D(O_j) P(O_j | A_i)\nprovided P(A_i)>0. Choice: A_i ≽_EDT A_k iff V(A_i)≥V(A_k). If ties, arbitrary. If acts not exhaustive? Use partition.\nIf outcomes determined by states S: O_j = f(A_i,S). Then V(A_i)=Σ_s P(S=s | A_i) U(A_i,s). The conditional P(S|A) can differ from P(S). EDT's central move is to let choice update beliefs about states via evidential dependence. In standard Savage causal expected utility, you might use P(S) independent of acts (states are probabilistically independent). EDT rejects requiring act-state independence; acts can be evidence of states.\n\nBut in decision theory, one worry: if agent can choose freely, should P(A) be positive? If agent's deliberation determines A, conditioning on A might be weird because she knows she will choose what she judges best. This can lead to Egan-style cases? Maybe not necessary. But mention zero probability.\n\nCDT formula: U(A)=Σ_j P(A □→ O_j)D(O_j). In causal Bayes net, U(A)=Σ_j P(O_j | do(A))D(O_j). If A is causally independent of O but correlated via common cause, P(O|A)≠P(O|do(A)). EDT uses observation, CDT uses intervention.\n\nCounterfactuals: EDT's counterfactual? It can be expressed as \"if I were to learn that A was chosen, what would my expected utility be?\" This is not necessarily closest-world intervention. If action probability zero, one can interpret via conditional desirability: Jeffrey's desirability V(A)=Des(A∧O)? Need maybe not go deep.\n\nPolicy evaluation:\nA policy π maps histories to actions. Let [π] be proposition \"agent implements π\". Then V(π)=E[D(O)|π]. If policy includes future actions, condition on all those actions. If policies are correlated with states/predictors, same evidential logic. In game theory, if multiple agents use similar algorithms, conditioning on own policy can be evidence of others' policies (superrationality). Source mentions twin PD and supporting argument.\n\nImportant open questions:\n1. Which probabilities? EDT needs a joint distribution including one's own action. In self-referential/predictor cases, how to set? Operational proposal: model decision algorithm as a variable D, predictor P, state S. Assign prior based on empirical/logical uncertainty. Then compute observational conditionals. If exact deterministic algorithm makes P(A) 0 or 1, add noise/trembles or condition on soft evidence. Justification: real agents are never logically omniscient and predictors are imperfect; limits recover ideal cases.\n2. Ratifiability: If an EDT agent conditions on the fact that she is choosing EDT, some choices may change value. A choice is ratifiable if it maximizes V conditional on the agent using EDT (or on the chosen decision rule). This can resolve some unstable cases; I propose using ratifiable EDT choices when simple maximization is self-undermining. Need mention source does not discuss.\n3. Dynamic inconsistency: Ex ante policy vs act-by-act. EDT may endorse precommitment to policies that later seem suboptimal? Or vice versa. Need propose: For binding policies, evaluate V(π) ex ante; for nonbinding sequential choice, evaluate each choice with updated conditional; if conflict, specify whether agent can commit.\n\nExamples in detail:\nNewcomb:\nLet T = take only B, B2 = take both. Predictor accuracy p. Let M=box B has million. Source assumes P(M|T)=p, P(M|B2)=1-p. Utilities money linear. V(T)=p*1,000,000. V(B2)=(1-p)*1,001,000 + p*1,000. For p=.99: 990k vs 11k. EDT one-box. Threshold p>0.5005. Note CDT: since prediction already made, P(M □→ T?) Actually two-boxing causally adds $1k regardless of box B, so CDT two-boxes. This illustrates EDT counts predictor correlation as reason.\n\nNeed maybe mention if predictor perfect: V(T)=1M, V(B2)=1k.\n\nTwin PD:\nPerfect twin: V(C)=5, V(D)=1. Cooperate. Imperfect correlation q threshold 5/7. Shows how to apply when dependency less than perfect. If correlation due to independent random decisions q=0.5? Then V(C)=2.5, V(D)=5.5? Actually q=.5: V(C)=2.5; V(D)=10-4.5=5.5, defect. EDT only cooperates if high enough correlation. This helps unfamiliar cases. Need distinguish source assumes exactly analogous reasoning gives probability 1. But what if not perfect? My extension.\n\nSmoking lesion (added):\nL lesion causes both smoking and cancer, no causal link smoke->cancer. Suppose no pleasure. P(C|S)=0.9, P(C|¬S)=0.1, cancer utility -100, no cancer 0. V(S)=0.9(-100)+0.1(0)=-90; V(¬S)=0.1(-100)+0.9(0)=-10. EDT says don't smoke, even though smoking does not cause cancer. CDT with do(S) gives same cancer rates, so indifferent or if pleasure positive smoke. This is the classic criticism: EDT may avoid bad news by refraining from symptoms. But in Newcomb/twin cases, one might argue dependencies are not mere symptoms? EDT doesn't internally distinguish unless supplemented with causal info. This is important.\n\nSleeping Beauty:\nMaybe a paragraph. Need not too long. Source says EDT applied to avoid Dutch books; halfers perhaps still vulnerable. I can state: EDT is not by itself a theory of indexical credence; to apply, one must specify probability of awakening/coin. If Beauty makes bets, EDT evaluates bet offers conditional on awakening; different credences (1/2 vs 1/3) lead to different verdicts. Source notes debate. This shows open boundary.\n\nPhilosophical:\nEDT as \"do what you most want to learn that you will do\". It treats choice as evidence. In a world with predictors, copies, correlated algorithms, choosing an act can be evidence of many similar choices; stakes higher (Evidentialist's Wager). But Lewis says managing news not world-making. The central dispute: whether rational choice should track causal production or information. EDT's answer: if you care about outcomes, and action is reliable evidence of outcome, conditional expected utility measures the value of the news. Critics reply: you can't eat news; you should cause good outcomes.\n\nNeed maybe mention expected utility axioms? Not necessary. But could say EDT is not an alternative to expected utility but a specification of probabilities.\n\nNeed ensure word count. Let's estimate. If final with headings maybe ~2600. Need maybe not too many references. But references count in words. Could be 2500.\n\nNeed produce final answer only. Need maybe include equations in LaTeX. Ensure readability.\n\nPotential pitfalls: Source says formula with P(O_j | A) D(O_j), D desirability. Need not confuse with utility of actions. Need maybe mention if outcome utilities include action itself, formula can be generalized.\n\nNeed discuss \"counterfactuals\" more explicitly: EDT conditionals are material conditional conditioning? There is a distinction: P(O|A) vs P(A □→ O). In Gibbard/Harper, CDT uses counterfactual probabilities. EDT uses conditional probabilities. But what about if action is known not to occur? Counterfactual conditional might differ. EDT's \"if I were to do A\" can be understood as conditional on the proposition A, not intervention. However if A has probability zero due to deterministic intention, ordinary conditioning fails. Need propose operational interpretation: use Jeffrey's radical probabilism? Maybe \"conditional desirability\" defined directly. For practical use, assign small positive probability to all acts (epsilon randomization), compute, then take limit. This is reasonable. Need include.\n\nNeed maybe mention dominance: EDT can violate causal dominance: Newcomb two-boxing dominates one-boxing causally? In Newcomb, if B fixed, two-box yields $1k more than one-box in each state (predicted one-box: two-box 1,001,000 vs one-box 1,000,000; predicted two-box: two-box 1,000 vs one-box 0). Yet EDT one-boxes. This highlights. Could include.\n\nNeed perhaps discuss policies in Newcomb: EDT might choose policy \"one-box\" before prediction? If predictor predicts policy. Same result. If agent can precommit to two-box after? Predictor may predict. EDT evaluates precommitment by conditional outcome. If precommitment changes predictor, etc.\n\nNeed maybe include \"relevant dependencies\" definition: dependency is relevant if it survives conditioning on all available information. That is, use posterior probability after evidence, but before action. If a dependency is explained away by a common cause? Need maybe: compute conditional probabilities relative to agent's total evidence E: V_E(A)=Σ P(O_j | A, E)D(O_j). This is important. In real cases, include all evidence. For example, if you know you have lesion, smoking no longer evidence of cancer? Actually if know lesion, P(C|S,L)=P(C|L) maybe; EDT would not avoid smoking due to cancer. So EDT's verdict depends on what is conditioned on. Need include. That helps operational. Formula with total evidence E:\nV(A)=Σ_j P(O_j | A, E) D(O_j). The source omits E but assumed. Need mention as my elaboration/standard. This is important for unfamiliar cases: include all background evidence, then condition on action. If you know common cause, correlation may vanish.\n\nNeed also discuss acts as propositions. Need maybe avoid too formal.\n\nNeed include references: Wikipedia, Gibbard & Harper, Ahmed, Lewis, Joyce, MacAskill et al. Maybe Conitzer. But avoid lengthy bibliography. Maybe list 6 references.\n\nNeed ensure not plagiarize source but paraphrase. Use own account.\n\nLet's start drafting final.\n\nPossible title: \"Evidential Decision Theory: Expected Utility as News\".\n\nOpening: \"Evidential decision theory (EDT) is a version of expected-utility theory in which the value of an act is its conditional expected desirability...\" quote source. Then mention source.\n\nNeed maybe state \"The core definition and examples below follow the Wikipedia revision; extensions are marked.\" Good.\n\nDetailed draft:\n\n---\n\nEvidential decision theory...\n\n1. Core idea\n\nWhen agent receives news \"you did A\", how good would world seem? EDT says choose act maximizing that posterior expected utility. Motto from Ahmed via Wikipedia: do what you most want to learn you will do. It contrasts with CDT... etc.\n\n2. Formal definition\n\nProbability space etc. Include total evidence E.\n\nLet acts A_i partition, outcomes O_j. Agent's probability P(·|E), desirability D. Define:\nV_E(A_i)=Σ_j P(O_j | A_i, E) D(O_j) = E[D|A_i,E]. If P(A_i|E)=0, conditional undefined; see §4.\n\nChoice rule: choose argmax. If continuous integrals. State form: V(A)=Σ_s P(S=s|A,E) U(A,s). Note states not independent.\n\nCDT formula: U(A)=Σ_j P(A □→ O_j | E) D(O_j), or do. Difference: observational vs interventional.\n\n3. Dependencies and counterfactuals\n\nEDT counts any probabilistic dependence given evidence: causal, predictive, common cause, logical/correlated reasoning. If A and O share common cause, conditionals track symptom. It treats \"counterfactual\" as conditionalization: hypothetical supposition A. In causal Bayes net, EDT is P(O|A,E), CDT is P(O|do(A),E). This makes clear.\n\nExample smoking lesion. Include math. Show EDT may choose symptom management. This is source not mention; my addition. This also illustrates why critics object. But EDT can respond? Maybe if action is caused by lesion, \"not smoking\" may also be evidence? Actually if lesion causes smoking, not smoking evidence no lesion. EDT values no smoking. If you know lesion status, correlation disappears. Include.\n\n4. Open operational questions\n\n- Probabilities over own actions. Need assign joint P(A,O). Since action is chosen, not observed randomly. Source leaves this. Proposal: model agent's decision process and predictors as variables; assign probabilities to possible choices as uncertain events from deliberative perspective. Use small trembles for zero-prob acts. Or conditional probabilities primitive.\n- Total evidence and self-information. Conditioning on \"I am an EDT agent choosing A\" can change values. Ratifiability: choose A only if V(A | I am using EDT and choose A?) Hmm. Need formulate carefully.\nMaybe: Let R be proposition \"I am reasoning according to EDT\". A ratifiable EDT choice satisfies A∈argmax V(A' | R,E). In some problems, unqualified EDT may choose an act that, once recognized as chosen by EDT, loses value; ratifiability filters. This is my proposal. Need not overcomplicate.\n- Policies. Policy π evaluated by V(π)=E[D|π,E]. For sequential: if policy binding, evaluate ex ante; if not, each choice re-evaluated; dynamic inconsistency possible. Reasonable operational interpretation: evaluate the object actually under agent's control (one-shot act vs commitment policy). If deciding policy, condition on policy adoption; if deciding immediate act, condition on act. Source doesn't resolve.\n\n5. Examples\n\nNewcomb. Full. Need maybe use p general. Compute threshold. Include CDT reasoning.\n\nTwin prisoner's dilemma. Full and q threshold.\n\nMaybe Sleeping Beauty short.\n\n6. Philosophical stakes\n\nLewis/Joyce criticisms, supporting arguments MacAskill. EDT as managing news vs causing. In correlated world, news may be causally irrelevant but decision may coordinate with copies. Need mention \"Evidentialist's Wager\" source: even if less credence, EDT may have higher stakes because correlated decision-makers. But be cautious: source says may be reasonable to act as if EDT true because stakes higher. We can summarize.\n\n7. Conclusion.\n\nReferences.\n\nNeed word count. Let's write with maybe 2500-2800. Need not mention word count. Ensure no weird markdown issues. Use LaTeX. Need maybe include equations with align.\n\nLet's draft in final. Need keep precise. Let's count rough as I write. Maybe final about 2700 words.\n\nDetailed content:\n\nTitle and intro 200 words.\nFormal definition 600.\nDependencies 500.\nOperational 500.\nExamples 800.\nPhilosophy 300.\nConclusion/references 300. Total 3200? Need keep concise. Target 2400-3000. Need maybe 2700.\n\nLet's draft carefully.\n\nPotential issue: In Newcomb threshold formula, if predictor accuracy p = P(predict one-box | one-box) = P(predict two-box | two-box). Then V(one)=p*1M + (1-p)*0? Wait if predictor predicts one-box then B contains M. If you one-box but predictor predicted two-box (prob 1-p), B empty and you get 0. Yes V1=p*1M. If two-box: if predictor predicted one-box (prob? P(pred one|two)=1-p if symmetric), B contains M and you get M+1000. If predicted two (prob p), B empty and get 1000. So V2=(1-p)(M+1000)+p*1000 = (1-p)M+1000. Good. Threshold pM > (1-p)M+1000 => (2p-1)M > 1000. With M=1,000,000, p > .5005. Good. Source uses p=.99.\n\nTwin PD q threshold: Define q=P(twin cooperates | I cooperate)=P(twin defects | I defect) (symmetry). Then V(C)=5q +0(1-q)=5q. V(D)=10(1-q)+1q = 10-9q. C if q>10/14=5/7. If perfect q=1, C. If q=0.5, D. Good.\n\nSmoking lesion math: Let C cancer, S smoke. Utilities: U(no cancer)=0, U(cancer)=-100; smoking no direct pleasure. Suppose P(C|S)=0.9, P(C|¬S)=0.1. V(S)=-90, V(¬S)=-10. EDT no smoke. If direct pleasure +20, V(S)=-70, still no smoke; if +100 maybe indifferent. CDT: if do(S) doesn't change cancer, V do smoke = direct pleasure + expected cancer based on lesion? Need not compute.\n\nSleeping Beauty: brief. Could say source states EDT used to avoid Dutch books but Conitzer argues halfers still vulnerable. EDT alone doesn't settle. Need maybe not too detailed.\n\nPolicy and counterfactual section maybe after examples? Could do before examples? Let's structure:\n1 Cor